        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 10,
        "titulo": "Numbers 11 to 20 and age with avoir",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: 0 to 10: zéro, un, deux, trois, quatre, cinq, six, sept, huit, neuf, dix. STEP 2: 11 to 16 are special forms: onze, douze, treize, quatorze, quinze, seize. STEP 3: 17 to 19 add up: dix-sept, dix-huit, dix-neuf. 20 is vingt. STEP 4: before a word that begins with a vowel, the final consonant is pronounced and linked: «deux amis» (duh-zah-MEE), «trois amis» (trwah-zah-MEE). Before a consonant, the end of «cinq, six, huit, dix» is usually dropped: «six garçons» (see gar-SOHN). STEP 5: to say your age, French uses «avoir» (to have), never «être»: «J'ai vingt ans.» STEP 6: to ask: «Quel âge as-tu ?» (informal) or «Quel âge avez-vous ?» (formal). STEP 7: «ans» is the plural of «an»: «un an, deux ans».",
        "comparacao": "In English we say «I am twenty» with «to be», and that is the classic mistake: «je suis vingt ans» is wrong. French says \"I have twenty years\": «j'ai vingt ans». You won't go wrong if you think \"have\", and French writes «j'ai».",
